le quotes surrounding the string.\n- In `raise ValueError(\"Inconsistent column count in matrix\")`\n- In the return dict keys and values: `\"operator_result\"`, `\"degrees\"`, and also the values `operator_result` and `degrees` are variables, no quotes. But the keys are strings, so we have double quotes.\n\nWe have escaped those. So the string is correct.\n\nNow we need to put this entire string as the value of \"implementation\" in a JSON object. The outer JSON object will have one key \"implementation\". So we write: {\"implementation\": \" ... \"}. The inner string must be escaped properly.\n\nWe'll construct the JSON manually, ensuring all quotes inside are escaped, and newlines as \\n, and also escape backslashes? There are no backslashes except the ones we inserted for escaping quotes. In the JSON string, a backslash is an escape character, so we need to double the backslash for each literal backslash in the string. Wait, careful: In the Python source code, we have literal backslash in the escaped quotes: `\\\"`. That is a backslash followed by a quote. In the JSON string representation, to include a literal backslash, we must escape it as `\\\\`. So every backslash in the source string must be written as `\\\\`. For example, the string `\\\"operator_result\\\"` in the source becomes `\\\\\"operator_result\\\\\"`? No, we need to think: The source string contains the characters: backslash, double-quote, o, p, e, r, a, t, o, r, _, r, e, s, u, l, t, backslash, double-quote. That sequence must be present inside the JSON string value. But when we put that sequence inside a JSON string, the backslash character must be escaped as `\\\\`. So we replace each `\\` with `\\\\`. So `\\\"` becomes `\\\\\"`. That means in the JSON string we will see `\\\\\"operator_result\\\\\"`. Similarly for other escapes.\n\nAlso the newlines: we wrote `\\n` in the line breaks, but in the actual JSON string, we want the newline character? Actually we are representing the Python source a